Confirmed the locking constraint: `mxfs_dir_drain_evict_data_blocks` takes `i_lock` internally, so it can't be called from `modify_refresh` (which holds `ILOCK_EXCL` → self-deadlock), and any `log_force`/drain under `ILOCK_EXCL` re-extends the EX hold → same DLM-timeout. This is a genuine architectural wall: the merge class is refuted 3×, in-hold drain is locking-blocked + timeout-risky, and prior acquire-drain reverts were on SCST (this is LIO/coherent). I have a complete PROVEN diagnosis. Per RULE 5 (GPT first), let me consult on the architecture.
GPT's design confirms and refines my direction. The key insight I was missing: **shift the pin-drain wait to NL/the fresh-grant boundary (where waiting doesn't block a peer), never in-hold; poison pin-only stale buffers so they're never an RMW base or xfsaild target; require purge-before-remodify.** The existing `mxfs_dir_drain_evict_data_blocks` runs at the slow-path acquire but the modify-path uses the clean-only evict (the gap). Let me read the drain loop's current wait bound to strengthen it (waiting at a fresh EX grant is safe — no peer blocked yet).
